# Schema authoring and target rules

The model is one layer, not three:

```text
TypedDict schema declarations (Markers/Patches/Segments/Subjects/Tracks)
  + frozen dataclasses (Marker/Patch/Segment/Subject)
 -> bind_scene(...) path-binds          (targets + geometry)
 -> MocapTrack.from_unbagged(...) data-binds     (MocapTrack[SubjectsT])
 -> demo.tracks[...] / mocap.subjects[...].segments[...].markers[...]
 -> MarkerTarget / PatchTarget / SegmentTarget   (stable string keys)
```

There is no separate `SegmentSpec`/`SceneView`/handle layer and no identifier
enums.

## Package-provided primitives

```python
class Markers(TypedDict): ...
class Patches(TypedDict): ...
class Segments(TypedDict): ...
class Subjects(TypedDict): ...

@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)
class Marker:
    mocap_name: str
    position_segment: Vec3 | None = field(default=None, compare=False)
    # private non-init _binding links to loaded data

@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)
class Patch:
    label: str
    transform_segment_patch: RigidTransform | None = ...
    region: ContactRegion | None = ...
    frame: str | None = None

@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)
class Segment[MarkersT: Markers, PatchesT: Patches]:
    markers: MarkersT
    patches: PatchesT
    mocap_name: str | None = None

@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)
class Subject[SegmentsT: Segments]:
    segments: SegmentsT
    mocap_name: str | None = None
```

Use PEP 695 syntax. Do not add identifier enums or `TypeVar` aliases.

## User/project schemas

```python
class ShoeMarkers(Markers):
    heel: Marker
    toe: Marker

class ShoePatches(Patches):
    sole: Patch
    toe_contact: Patch

class ShoeSegments(Segments):
    shoe: Segment[ShoeMarkers, ShoePatches]

class MocapSubjects(Subjects):
    left_shoe: Subject[ShoeSegments]
    right_shoe: Subject[ShoeSegments]
```

Reuse one schema for structurally identical subjects (e.g. both shoes).

## Patches

Patches are siblings of markers under a segment. `Patch(label=...)` is
declaration-only (targetable, but `.points()`/`.normals()` raise once bound).
`Patch.rectangle(markers=..., width=..., height=..., outward_axis=...,
forward_axis=...)` fits the patch frame at bind time from the named markers'
segment-frame positions (from the subject `body_model` or per-marker
`position_segment`); `outward_axis`/`forward_axis` are required, `normal_offset`
defaults to `0.0`. For an already-known frame, pass `transform_segment_patch`
and a `RectangularRegion` to the base `Patch(...)` constructor. The bind-time
fit is performed by the `calibrate_patch_transform(...)` primitive, which
backends may also call directly.

## Runtime traversal and queries

```python
scene = bind_scene(subjects)                    # path-bound, dataless
shoe = scene["left_shoe"].segments["shoe"]
heel_target = shoe.marker_target("heel")
sole_target = shoe.patch_target("sole")

mocap = demo.tracks["mocap"]                      # bound to data
shoe = mocap.subjects["left_shoe"].segments["shoe"]
shoe.markers["heel"].positions()                 # single
shoe.marker_positions("heel", "toe", as_dict=True)  # batch, string-keyed
shoe.patches["sole"].points()
shoe.translations()
```

## Stable targets

```python
SegmentTarget(subject="left_shoe", segment="shoe")
MarkerTarget(subject="left_shoe", segment="shoe", marker="heel")
PatchTarget(subject="left_shoe", segment="shoe", patch="sole")
```

Contact tracks key by `PatchTarget`; mocap pose state by `SegmentKey`. Do not
reintroduce handles or enum-typed identity. Authored strings are public field
names; targets/keys are the runtime identity mechanism.
